   140	
   141	## Amendment 3 (2026-11-03): RMM transferable-extension test (publishability addendum)
   142	
   143	Registered before any sprint validation row was read. The test uses fresh seeds, so it is independent
   144	of the sprint validation rows. Every earlier RMM result predates the DISCERN PrPl-EB `/4` fix. The
   145	`rmm:discern_style+RMM` factory now wraps the corrected `DiscernStyleStop`.
   146	
   147	**HR (transfer).**
   148	* Hosts: gated_deployment, discern_style (corrected), SMCS+retain-incumbent, model_picker_adapter and
   149	  cams_adapter, each also run with RMM attached.
   150	* Primary family, the change-and-stationary-improvement regimes the RMM claim was made on:
   151	  {V_late_600, W_late_1000, V_better_a, V_better_b, V_better_c, V_near}.
   152	* Statistic: the per-seed family mean of J(h+RMM) - J(h).
   153	* Seeds: 100 fresh synthetic seeds, base 2026110606.
   154	* HR passes if the 95% CI is below 0 for at least 4 of the 5 hosts, and first-promotion safety holds
   155	  under the iid conditional null. Safety means the Wilson upper bound is at most .0936 on V_null and
   156	  V_worse over 1000 seeds (base 2026110607).
   157	* The ρ=.8/.95 Markov nulls are reported as known out-of-scope failures. RMM's validity is stated
   158	  under the iid conditional null only.
   159	
   160	**HR2 (versus strong baselines, descriptive plus one test).** The same family mean is reported for the
   161	best host+RMM against:
   162	* gated_adaptive_range+front
   163	* gated_deployment+QCE+front
   164	* xu_acquisition_only
   165	* discern_design_floor
   166	* P7s_settled
   167	* A5b
   168	* the best certified published comparator, chosen per regime
   169	
   170	The one test asks whether the best host+RMM is significantly cheaper than the best certified published
   171	comparator on {V_late_600, W_late_1000}. Holm correction is applied over the two regimes.
   172	
   173	**Attribution.**
   174	* +RMM_single, which drops the restart mixture.
   175	* +RMM_noacq, which drops continued acquisition.
   176	
   177	## Publication track
   178	The publishability addendum (2026-11-03) keeps two separate assessments, main-track contribution
   179	potential and SOTA performance. They are recorded in `publication_register.md` and in the leaderboard.
   180	They do not change any registered hypothesis above.
   181	
   182	## Amendment 4 (2026-11-03): transfer of the D deploy-gate (registered after reading the validation results)
   183	
   184	This test is registered **after** the sprint validation and real-data results were read: on the fresh real
   185	streams, D2 (CAMS plus the gate) was the only candidate that beat the best certified published comparator
   186	on most datasets. It is therefore a confirmation test of that observation. It runs on new synthetic seeds
   187	and on the fresh real streams.
   188	
   189	**HD (transfer of one gate across three ranking hosts).**
   190	* The gate uses D1's parameter set. It is attached to three hosts: Model Picker (`D1`), CAMS (`D2`) and
   191	  Model Selector (`D6-D1params:ms`).
   192	* For each host, these quantities are computed:
   193	  * (i) J(gated) - J(native host), paired, over all VAL regimes, the nulls included.
   194	  * (ii) J(gated) - J(best certified published), on the 5 iid alternatives, Holm-corrected.
   195	  * (iii) false promotion on the iid nulls V_null and V_worse, plus V_null_dep_r5, from 1000 fresh seeds.
   196	* Seeds: synthetic 100 at base 2026110608; nulls 1000 at base 2026110609; fresh real data at 20 seeds,
   197	  base 2026110610.
   198	* HD passes when **all three hosts** meet both of these:
   199	  * (iii): the Wilson upper bound is at most .0936 on V_null, V_worse and V_null_dep_r5.
   200	  * (ii): at least 3 of 5 Holm-significant improvements.
   201	* Scope: the iid / weakly dependent conditional null. The rho >= .8 failure is a known out-of-scope limitation.
===== docs/sota_sprint/fresh_real_protocol.md =====
     1	# SOTA sprint: fresh real-data external validation protocol
     2	
     3	Registered 2026-11-03 by the benchmarking/data agent. The datasets, pair specification, episode
     4	plan, label delays and analysis below were fixed **before any ShiftLab policy, candidate, or the
     5	screen runner touched these data**. Only the frozen incumbent/challenger models were fit (to
     6	produce the loss streams). The descriptive statistics in
     7	`data/sprint_fresh_losses/README.md` are evaluator-side and must not be used to tune, select or
     8	re-specify any policy, contract parameter or analysis choice.
     9	
    10	None of the five datasets has been used in ShiftLab before (previously used: airlines, bank,
    11	criteo, elec2, rialto, spam, NOAA weather, openml_ldpa, uci_occupancy). No substitutions were
    12	needed: all five suggested downloads succeeded (total about 35 MB).
    13	
    14	## Datasets
    15	
    16	All from the UCI Machine Learning Repository, license CC BY 4.0. Raw zips are kept under
    17	`data/raw/sprint_fresh/uci_<id>/raw.zip` (gitignored).
    18	
    19	| dataset | UCI id | URL | raw zip sha256 | span | rows n |
    20	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
    21	| metro | 492 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/492/metro+interstate+traffic+volume.zip | b99aeabcbd6cc86f642da3a79d90883425798f58abd3b3302da2fa19dda73768 | 2012-10 to 2018-09, hourly | 48204 |
    22	| beijing_pm25 | 381 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/381/beijing+pm2+5+data.zip | 40cdd29c24918e855133dce280ff59779bb0b5852898e05bafcf422a4cc0a175 | 2010-01 to 2014-12, hourly | 41757 |
    23	| bike_hour | 275 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/275/bike+sharing+dataset.zip | b70182d0d0508e9abbb79306ce5c0cec34869000f8220175ac83d11dbe845401 | 2011-01 to 2012-12, hourly | 17379 |
    24	| appliances | 374 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/374/appliances+energy+prediction.zip | 2fccf354445d886e7917620b0195db1f3e3e34d5a067a93b844694a4c561255a | 2016-01 to 2016-05, 10-min | 19735 |
    25	| household_power | 235 | https://archive.ics.uci.edu/static/public/235/individual+household+electric+power+consumption.zip | 9f84b46ade8a2d8e1286ec4b2b6c2987a45a755c59f263be3b3b3d10dfbda3ff | 2006-12 to 2010-11, 10-min means | 204950 |
    26	
    27	Derived loss caches (`data/sprint_fresh_losses/<dataset>.npz`, arrays `l0`, `l1` int8 (K, 2000),
    28	`starts`):
    29	
    30	| npz | file sha256 | array_content_sha256 |
    31	|---|---|---|
    32	| metro.npz | d2e44130d052f1e73a38c6e2b8d731d1c9bef5b955b0447c14c1a5998788bdb1 | fcffeb091836f64753acd1d59f1cb7d3cb9d698c2b1671c8899e584138bafb7d |
    33	| beijing_pm25.npz | b29ea4991e25abdd3160399e597d56ce08d786da7a6fd6a6439f4cdb5065a4aa | 5e162db728f5b3a87f98c8712bc2f188c39acb5c771a44d2b768cc8d33a6cdf0 |
    34	| bike_hour.npz | 6572177367e66927859e6a64e189ae41402bdc262d96f480490977f87c73fb66 | 9e00cebfd33996f4ed6f0db719e28d7f7a20c234d7696329a38c464b530655db |
    35	| appliances.npz | 03e7a8b93a55a4efe5a6cef5af25b16aa88f3b73c03bde6c2444037a543af734 | 4e86c780a1c16c3334517bee634e76aadd23bf2a4ce94213dbf1b5abbfdced51 |
    36	| household_power.npz | e72049ab25102e9192b4c1ad43c73ee2fffb66e6dcba30da27a96eee72c6a582 | e658a1f8e3a8ff6dcb9edc8e9594ea77207bb9a58dd36e32f905a251f7433983 |
    37	
    38	The file hash changes on rebuild because `np.savez` stamps zip entry times; verify rebuilds with
    39	`array_content_sha256` = sha256(l0.tobytes() + l1.tobytes() + starts.tobytes()).
    40	
    41	## Pair specification
    42	
    43	Mirrors `scripts/promising_directions/b3_real_data.py::build_fetched`; code in
    44	`scripts/sota_sprint/fresh_real.py` (sklearn 1.9.1, numpy 2.5.4, pandas 3.0.6).
    45	
    46	* Rows sorted chronologically; n rows after cleaning; n15 = int(0.15n), n30 = int(0.30n).
    47	* Label y = 1[target > median of target on rows [0, n30)].
    48	* Train-only preprocessing on rows [0, n30): StandardScaler (numeric) and
    49	  OneHotEncoder(min_frequency=20, handle_unknown="ignore") (categorical).
    50	* Incumbent pi_0: LogisticRegression(max_iter=300, C=1.0) on rows [0, n15).
    51	* Challenger pi_1: HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=100, max_depth=4, learning_rate=0.1,
    52	  random_state=0) on rows [0, n30).
    53	* Losses l_k = 1[pi_k(x) != y]. Both are observed for every row once the label arrives, so the
    54	  paired loss difference is identifiable without an off-policy design.
    55	* Per-dataset target, leakage drops and features are listed in `data/sprint_fresh_losses/README.md`.
    56	  Leakage drops: bike casual/registered; household Global_intensity and Sub_metering_1-3.
    57	  Timestamps are dropped after deriving hour, weekday and month.
    58	
    59	## Episode plan
    60	
    61	K = min(20, floor((n - n30)/2000)) consecutive non-overlapping windows of T = 2000 rows starting
    62	at n30. `starts` in each npz gives absolute row indices.
    63	
    64	| dataset | n30 | K | last row used |
    65	|---|---|---|---|
    66	| metro | 14461 | 16 | 46461 |
    67	| beijing_pm25 | 12527 | 14 | 40527 |
    68	| bike_hour | 5213 | 6 | 17213 |
    69	| appliances | 5920 | 6 | 17920 |
    70	| household_power | 61485 | 20 | 101485 |
    71	
    72	Total 62 episodes. The household_power windows after row 101485 are not used.
    73	
    74	## Label delays (registered, in events; `DELAYS` in fresh_real.py)
    75	
    76	One day of natural time: metro 24, beijing_pm25 24, bike_hour 24, appliances 144,
    77	household_power 144. Metro has 7629 rows that share an hour with another row (one row per weather
    78	description), so 24 events is somewhat less than 24 hours there. Delays are fixed and in event
    79	order; no missing or out-of-order labels are simulated.
    80	
    81	## Analysis the lead will run
    82	
    83	* Contract: T = 2000, B = 200, c_q = .05, c_s = 5, label delay as registered above.
    84	* Policy seeds: 20 per episode at seed base 2026110604. Policy randomness is averaged within an
    85	  episode; the episode is the unit of inference.
    86	* Primary statistic per dataset and contrast: episode-level paired Delta J = J(candidate) -
    87	  J(comparator), mean over episodes with a 95% t-interval (df = K - 1). Also pooled over all 62
    88	  episodes, and reported per dataset.
    89	* Because episodes within a dataset are consecutive windows of one series, they are dependent and
    90	  the t-intervals are optimistic. Report, in addition: the fraction of episodes with Delta J < 0,
    91	  and a per-dataset two-sided exact sign test on episode Delta J (ties dropped).
    92	* Report promotion time and false promotions (switching on episodes where the challenger's
    93	  episode mean loss is not lower) as descriptive outcomes. Note that 56 of 62 episodes favour the
    94	  challenger, so these streams are mainly a test of cost of promoting a better challenger; harmful
    95	  switches are only possible on 6 episodes (5 household_power, 1 beijing_pm25).
    96	* Market or calendar dates are not causal change labels; no change times are inferred.
    97	
    98	## Status
    99	
   100	Smoke-level build only. No policy has been run on these streams as of registration.
===== docs/sota_sprint/benchmark_spec.md =====
     1	# SOTA sprint: frozen benchmark specification (Agent E)
     2	
     3	Registered 2026-11-03 on branch `research/sota-sprint-24` (from `origin/main` 2171689) before any
     4	sprint run. Code: [scripts/sota_sprint/bench.py](../../scripts/sota_sprint/bench.py).
     5	
     6	* Contract: T=2000, hard B=200, c_q=.05, c_s=5, alpha=.05, release-before-act, delay >= 1, common
     7	  simulator `scripts/promising_directions/sota/simulator.py`. J = loss + .05 labels + 5 switches.
     8	* Paired streams: synthetic seed s of regime R = `SeedSequence([seed_base, sha(R), s])`; every policy
     9	  replays the same stream with a private generator.
    10	* Development screening: seed base 2026110501 (unused before), 20 seeds per environment, environments
    11	  from `DEV_ENVS` only (Atlas v2 F0-F9 + sprint additions S_* + budget variants `@B100`, `@B400`).
    12	* Validation (registered later, before any validation run): fresh seed base, 100 paired seeds, the
    13	  Atlas v2 V_* regimes plus new instances; null safety additionally on >= 500 null seeds.
    14	* Null-safety bound: promotion rate <= .0936 on every null regime incl. rho=.8 and rho=.95 Markov
    15	  nulls (Atlas v2 Amendment 2). 20-seed rates are screening diagnostics, not evidence of safety.
    16	* Comparators: `CERTIFIED_PUBLISHED` (strongest certified class, chosen per regime on the same seeds),
    17	  `UNCERTIFIED_PUBLISHED`, internal earlier candidates (QSG, QSG-env8, FLQ, RMM, LRMD, P7s; never
    18	  counted as published SOTA), controls. Author-faithful status per method is in
    19	  `docs/promising_directions/sota/SOTA_IMPLEMENTATION_VALIDATION.md`; every row here is a
    20	  common-contract port unless stated.
    21	* Primary statistic: paired Delta J = J(candidate) - J(comparator), 95% t-interval over seeds.
    22	* Runner/analysis: `scripts/sota_sprint/screen.py`, `scripts/sota_sprint/analyze.py`.
===== docs/sota_sprint/REPORT.md =====
     1	# ShiftLab SOTA sprint: execution report
     2	
     3	**Date:** 2026-11-03.
     4	**Branch:** `research/sota-sprint-24`, from `origin/main` 2171689.
     5	**Protocol:** [benchmark_spec.md](benchmark_spec.md) and [validation_protocol.md](validation_protocol.md).
     6	The protocol was locked before validation and amended four times. Amendments 1–3 were committed before
     7	any validation row was read. Amendment 4 was registered afterwards and is labelled post-hoc.
     8	**Contract:** T=2000, hard B=200, c_q=.05, c_s=5, α=.05, common simulator, paired streams.
     9	
    10	## 1. Mandatory execution gate (brief §14A): MET
    11	
    12	* **Implementation and screening.** All 24 candidates (A1–A6, B1–B6, C1–C6, D1–D6) were implemented,
    13	  unit-tested and screened. There are 102 tests in `tests/sota_sprint`.
    14	  * Per family: 9–12 diagnostic environments, 20 paired seeds, a null or adverse environment, and an
    15	    environment outside the intended region.
    16	  * Each candidate has a mechanism ablation, saved raw records, cost, efficacy and safety metrics, and a
    17	    disposition.
    18	* **Mechanical audit from raw records** ([gate_audit.json](gate_audit.json),
    19	  `scripts/sota_sprint/gate_audit.py --root outputs/sota_sprint/gate`).
    20	  * 24/24 PASS.
    21	  * The audit is family-isolated: a candidate is credited only with runs from its own family's
    22	    directories. This follows the ARIS milestone-1 audit finding that names were reused across families.
    23	  * The re-screen was run at the committed revision. It reproduces 36,696 of 37,364 (98.2%) of the
    24	    agents' original dev rows exactly. The remainder came from intermediate code revisions.
    25	* **Ledger and leaderboard.** [candidates.json](candidates.json) and [leaderboard.json](leaderboard.json)
    26	  hold the per-candidate dispositions. [leaderboard_views.md](leaderboard_views.md) has the seven ranked
    27	  views (§12), with development and validated results kept apart.
    28	* **Phase 7 combinations.**
    29	  * Built and screened: X_A5B5, X_A6B5, X_A5C, X_D4A1, X_D6A1:mp and X_D6A1:cams
    30	    ([combos/README.md](combos/README.md)).
    31	  * Only X_A5C met the advance rule.
    32	  * Not built, with reasons recorded: A2+C3, B5+C5, B6+A1 and D6+C2.
    33	
    34	| family | dispositions (development) |
    35	|---|---|
    36	| A, dependence-aware certification | A1, A5 promising (valid for any ρ); A2, A3 fast but unsafe; A4 valid, no power; A6 = A5 |
    37	| B, recovery | B2 promising vs its host (reversal); B1, B3–B6 negative |
    38	| C, acquisition economics | C2, C6 weak (label saving only; did not replicate); C1, C3–C5 negative |
    39	| D, safety for fast selectors | D1, D2, D6 promising (null control; transfer across 3 hosts); D3–D5 negative |
    40	
    41	## 2. Validation (fresh seeds; registered hypotheses)
    42	
    43	The full tables are in [validation/validation_results.md](validation/validation_results.md),
    44	[validation/real_results.md](validation/real_results.md) and
    45	[validation/transfer_results.md](validation/transfer_results.md).
    46	
    47	* **Seeds:** 100 per regime over 21 regimes (base 2026110601); 1000 per null over 7 in-class nulls and
    48	  2 out-of-class nulls.
    49	* **Real data:** 5 fresh UCI chronological datasets with 62 episodes. They were registered (aeb2d4f)
    50	  before any policy run. The 9 previously exposed streams are reported descriptively.
    51	
    52	| registered test | result |
    53	|---|---|
    54	| H0, the Atlas v2 stop rule (A5b vs Xu / tuned pacing; CI<0 on ≥ 4/6) | **FAIL** (1/6) |
    55	| H1, A5b class-scoped reliability, part 1 (safety, Wilson upper bound, 7 in-class nulls) | PASS (≤ .005) |
    56	| H1, part 2 (each certified comparator fails a null or is cost-dominated) | PASS (11/11) |
    57	| H1, part 3 (non-inferiority within +5 on ≥ 4/5 iid alternatives) | **FAIL** (1/5) |
    58	| H2, cost superiority with safety (A5b, A1b, D1, D2, C2, C6, X_A5C, B2, B5) | **FAIL** for all; D2 is cheaper (4/5 Holm) but unsafe at ρ ≥ .8 |
    59	| H4, combinations | only X_A5C advanced; its late-regime gain vs A5b is −0.5 / −0.9 |
    60	| HR (Amendment 3), RMM transfer to ≥ 4/5 hosts | **FAIL** (3/5; all 3 certificate hosts pass) |
    61	| HD (Amendment 4, post-hoc), deploy-gate transfer to 3/3 hosts | **FAIL** (Holm-significant 0/2/2 of 5; all safe on iid nulls) |
    62	
    63	## 3. Best mechanisms and the evidence for them
    64	
    65	1. **RMM on certificate hosts (gated, corrected DISCERN, SMCS).**
    66	   * Family cost vs host: −44.2, −47.3 and −13.3.
    67	   * Late improvement vs the best certified published method (Xu): −15 to −27 for every host.
    68	   * Independent-null safety holds.
    69	   * Attribution: the restart mixture contributes −2.4 to −5.5 and continued acquisition −8 to −11.
    70	   * Losses: reversal and recurrence +20 to +37; ρ=.8 null promotion .15.
    71	2. **A5b (gap-indexed sequential universal inference certificate plus adaptive density).**
    72	   * The only powered certified method that stays within the bound for every ρ ≤ .95. It also holds on
    73	     two out-of-class nulls (.048, .018).
    74	   * Stationary cost is within +7.4 of the cheapest unsafe certified port.
    75	   * It fails on real time-series data (+61 to +257). Measured real persistence is ρ̂ .4–.98.
    76	3. **Deploy gate on fast selectors.**
    77	   * Native null promotion of 64–100% falls to about .015 on the three hosts.
    78	   * D2 (CAMS) beats the best certified method on 4/5 fresh real datasets.
    79	   * Its synthetic superiority did not replicate on new seeds.
    80	
    81	## 4. Research verdicts
    82	
    83	* **SOTA: `SOTA_NOT_MET`.**
    84	  * No candidate satisfies every success-gate item (brief §14B) for a registered claim.
    85	  * The strongest result is RMM's late-improvement advantage on certificate hosts, which is real.
    86	    However, it rests on a post-hoc scope (certificate hosts only) after a registered 5-host test failed,
    87	    and it has no real-data late-change confirmation.
    88	* **Main-track candidates:** see the [publication register](publication_register.md) and §5.
    89	
    90	## 5. Independent reviews
    91	
    92	* **Milestones 1–2.** [review_milestone12.md](review_milestone12.md), by a native Claude reviewer. Its
    93	  objections O1–O14 were handled by Amendment 2 before any validation row was read.
    94	* **ARIS `experiment-audit`, milestone 1.** [aris/m1_EXPERIMENT_AUDIT.md](aris/m1_EXPERIMENT_AUDIT.md).
    95	  Run by Codex `gpt-reserve`, a different model family. Verdict FAIL on gate-audit name pooling, now
    96	  fixed.
    97	* **ARIS `experiment-audit` and `result-to-claim`, milestone 3.** In [aris/](aris/); see the addendum
    98	  below.
    99	* **Novelty.** [novelty_audit.md](novelty_audit.md).
   100	
   101	## 6. Reproduction
   102	
   103	    uv run pytest -q tests/sota_sprint
   104	    ./docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/run_validation.sh      # synthetic, nulls, B5, real, QCE, out-of-class
   105	    ./docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/run_rmm_transfer.sh    # Amendments 3 and 4
   106	    ./docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/run_gate.sh            # family-isolated gate re-screen
   107	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_validation.py
   108	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_real.py
   109	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/analyze_transfer.py
   110	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/gate_audit.py --root outputs/sota_sprint/gate
   111	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/leaderboard_views.py
   112	    uv run python scripts/sota_sprint/fresh_real.py   # rebuilds the fresh loss caches from UCI
   113	
   114	The run scripts are committed in `docs/sota_sprint/run_scripts/` (run from the repo root; `run_gate.sh` reads its policy/regime lists from `outputs/sota_sprint/`, copies of which are alongside); each run also records its argv in
   115	its `manifest.json`. Raw records stay local.
   116	
   117	## 7. Limitations
   118	
   119	* Every comparator is a common-contract port, not author code.
   120	* Development used 20 seeds.
   121	* Real episodes are consecutive windows, so their CIs are optimistic.
   122	* Four of the five part-3 regimes were exposed in Atlas v2, with other seeds.
   123	* Family C held-out seeds shared the null-check base.
   124	* The fresh real streams contain almost no harmful-switch episodes (6/62), so they mostly measure
   125	  promotion cost.
   126	* PaperBanana was not used; no figures were produced.
===== docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md =====
     1	# Publication Opportunity Register (SOTA sprint, 2026-11-03)
     2	
     3	This register follows the publishability addendum. Main-track contribution potential (A) and SOTA performance (B) are assessed separately.
     4	
     5	All numbers come from the sprint validation files in [validation/](validation/). Status uses three values: PROMISING, NEEDS EVIDENCE and NOT SUPPORTED. The result-to-claim review in [aris/](aris/) may revise these.
     6	
     7	---
     8	
     9	## P1. A restart-mixture label-clock monitor that transfers across certified deployment methods (RMM)
    10	
    11	* **Research question.** Certified (anytime-valid) deployment gates stop measuring once they reach a verdict, and they dilute late evidence with early evidence. Can a single attachable module fix late-improvement latency for any certificate host?
    12	* **Insight / mechanism.**
    13	  * The module runs a Shiryaev-Roberts-style restart mixture of label-clock e-processes. It works on query-conditional, unweighted outcomes.
    14	  * It keeps acquiring labels after the host's verdict.
    15	  * When it alarms, it switches deployment and restarts the host. The alpha is split between host and detector.
    16	* **Closest prior art.**
    17	  * E-detectors (Shin, Ramdas and Rinaldo); Saha and Ramdas.
    18	  * Data-efficient quickest change detection (Banerjee and Veeravalli).
    19	  * The certified hosts: Karampatziakis et al. 2021, DISCERN, SMCS.
    20	* **Exact difference.** The module attaches to an arbitrary certified deployment host, on the label clock, under a shared hard label budget. It continues acquiring after the verdict and restarts the host. No paper evaluates restart detectors as a deployment add-on across certificate families.
    21	* **Evidence.** All results are on fresh seeds, after the DISCERN `/4` fix ([transfer_results.md](validation/transfer_results.md), [rmm_certified_hosts.txt](validation/rmm_certified_hosts.txt)).
    22	  * Host+RMM minus host on the registered family:
    23	    * gated −44.2 [−46.9, −41.4]
    24	    * corrected DISCERN −47.3 [−49.8, −44.8]
    25	    * SMCS −13.3 [−16.0, −10.6]
    26	  * Independent-null first-promotion safety holds on all three hosts (Wilson upper bound ≤ .0936 at 1000 seeds).
    27	  * Late improvement vs the best certified published method (Xu) is −15 to −27 per episode for every certified host, on V_late_600 and W_late_1000.
    28	  * Family mean vs strong baselines (gated+front, QCE+front, P7s, A5b) is −2.4 to −7.3.
    29	  * Ablations: the restart mixture contributes −2.4 to −5.5 and continued acquisition −8 to −11.
    30	* **Adverse results.**
    31	  * Registered HR fails at 3/5 hosts. Model Picker and CAMS have no certificate for RMM to improve: their null promotion is .94 to .95 with or without RMM.
    32	  * Reversal and recurrence cost +20 to +37 vs the best certified method.
    33	  * Strong stationary improvement costs +9 to +34 vs front-loaded EB.
    34	  * The ρ=.8 Markov null gives .15 and ρ=.95 gives .66 to .70.
    35	  * On fresh real data RMM is much better than its host (gated: −48 to −156) but mixed vs the best certified method (2 wins, 3 losses). The fresh streams contain no late-improvement episode.
    36	* **Deviation to disclose.** Restricting the transfer claim to certificate hosts was decided after the registered 5-host HR failed. It is therefore a post-hoc scope, even though RMM's design rationale (adding monitoring to a certificate) predates it.
    37	* **Necessary additional evidence.**
    38	  1. A registered fresh test restricted to certificate hosts, adding a fourth certificate family (e.g. choe_ramdas_eb or gated_adaptive_range).
    39	  2. Real streams with a genuine within-stream improvement, e.g. a challenger retrained on recent data that overtakes the incumbent.
    40	  3. Fixing or bounding the reversal cost, with B2-style certified rollback.
    41	  4. A formal statement of the joint error of promote then rollback.
    42	* **Positioning.** A transferable extension (addendum type 1) and failure-guided design (type 4), with a scoped SOTA in the late-improvement regime (type 3).
    43	* **(A) Main-track potential:** moderate. Novelty is incremental (composition of e-detectors). The transfer across three independent certificate families plus the latency diagnosis gives it a coherent story.
    44	* **(B) SOTA:** scoped, synthetic only. Late improvement beats the strongest certified published method by 15 to 27; nothing broader.
    45	* **Assessment: PROMISING (needs evidence items 1 and 2).**
    46	
    47	## P2. Certified deployment under serial dependence: failure map and an impossibility-flavoured trade-off (Failure Atlas plus persistence study)
    48	
    49	* **Research question.** Do anytime-valid deployment gates keep their false-promotion guarantee when label outcomes are serially dependent, as they are in real time-series streams? What does a dependence-robust certificate cost?
    50	* **Main finding.**
    51	  * Every certified published port that has power exceeds the .0936 bound on a persistent marginal null. Run at 1000 seeds each:
    52	    * Xu: ρ=.9 .117, ρ=.95 .239
    53	    * SMCS: ρ=.8 .097, ρ=.95 .350
    54	    * gated_adaptive_range: ρ=.95 .112
    55	    * DISCERN floor: ρ=.95 .110
    56	    * Choe–Ramdas-style EB: ρ=.95 .213, front-loaded ρ=.8 .116
    57	    * QCE-betting: ρ=.95 .232
    58	  * The two that pass, original gated deployment and corrected DISCERN, almost never promote. A5b cost-dominates them on all 6 stationary alternatives.
    59	  * A gap-indexed sequential-universal-inference certificate (A1b/A5b) holds the bound for every ρ ≤ .95. It also holds on two out-of-class nulls (.048, .018). Its stationary cost is within +7.4 of the cheapest unsafe certified method.
    60	  * But it loses all power on real time-series streams. Fresh real data: +61 to +257 vs the best certified method. On metro it costs +99 with 0/16 episodes better, even though the challenger error is .047 vs .36.
    61	  * The mechanism is measured persistence: ρ̂ of .4 to .8 on the fresh streams, and up to .98 on exposed streams.
    62	  * This sits alongside the shared certification-latency diagnosis (M1, uniform pacing under a binding budget) and the churn of uncertified selectors (M2, 64 to 100% null promotion).
    63	* **Closest prior art.**
    64	  * Choe and Ramdas 2024; Henzi and Ziegel 2022. These control the time-averaged *conditional* mean, so a promotion in a persistent run is correct for their null.
    65	  * Universal inference (Wasserman, Ramdas and Balakrishnan 2020).
    66	  * Markov e-detectors.
    67	* **Exact difference.**
    68	  * The atlas measures marginal false promotion of deployment gates under controlled persistence, on one common budgeted, delayed simulator, against 13 published methods. It also shows the conditional-vs-marginal null distinction has decision consequences.
    69	  * A gap-indexed Markov UI certificate for actively queried outcomes; the novelty audit found no prior use of it.
    70	* **Necessary additional evidence.**
    71	  1. A short formal argument that, under unknown persistence, any test valid for all ρ ≤ 1 needs evidence that grows with run changes rather than with labels. This would turn the trade-off into a lower bound.
    72	  2. Real streams with a known null, e.g. two equally accurate retrained models, to measure real false-promotion rates.
    73	  3. Author-code comparators where available.
    74	* **Positioning.** A new evaluation methodology plus a substantive empirical finding (addendum types 5 and 6), possibly a datasets-and-benchmarks track.
    75	* **(A) Main-track potential:** moderate to good as an empirical paper, if the lower-bound argument holds.
    76	* **(B) SOTA:** A5b is the lowest-cost method among those that hold the bound at ρ ≤ .95 on synthetic data. It is not SOTA on real data.
    77	* **Assessment: PROMISING (needs evidence item 1).**
    78	
    79	## P3. A shared deploy-or-abstain gate for fast online model selectors (Family D)
    80	
    81	* **Research question.** Can fast uncertified selectors (Model Picker, CAMS, Model Selector) keep most of their economy once an anytime certificate is required before deployment?
    82	* **Mechanism.** The host ranks and queries. Promotion requires a started-mixture e-process on query-conditional outcomes. Gate queries are added only while the host is undecided.
    83	* **Evidence.**
    84	  * Native null promotion of 64 to 100% falls to .014 to .016 on V_null for all three hosts at 1000 seeds.
    85	  * Fresh real data, D2 (CAMS) vs the best certified method:
    86	    * beijing −33.2 (13/14 episodes)
    87	    * bike −6.1 (5/6)
    88	    * metro −2.7 (14/16)
    89	    * household −2.8 (11/20)
    90	    * appliances +1.4
    91	* **Adverse results.**
    92	  * Registered HD fails. Holm-significant wins vs the best certified method number 0, 2 and 2 of 5.
    93	  * D2's first-validation 4/5 did not replicate on new seeds.
    94	  * ρ=.8 null promotion is .20 to .23.
    95	  * Real gains are consistent only on the CAMS host.
    96	* **Closest prior art.** Composition of published gates (Karampatziakis et al. 2021) with published selectors. The novelty audit judged it composition.
    97	* **(A) Main-track potential:** low as a method. It is useful as the M2 benchmark finding inside P2.
    98	* **(B) SOTA:** not established.
    99	* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE, leaning NOT SUPPORTED as a standalone paper.**
   100	
   101	## P4. Certified rollback (B2/B5)
   102	
   103	* **Evidence.** Reversal cost vs host: B2 −8.9 and −8.4; B5 −12.0 and −17.8 (fresh seeds).
   104	* **Adverse results.**
   105	  * One host only, in the RMM line, which fails ρ=.8.
   106	  * Reversal is still +20 or more vs the best certified method (discern_style), because that method never promotes.
   107	* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE.** Best used as P1's fix for reversal cost.
   108	
   109	## P5. FLQ/QCE/QSG front-loading, adaptive density, shadow pricing (A2, A3, C1 to C6)
   110	
   111	* **Evidence.** Earlier Atlas v2 work: stationary gains bought with dependence-safety failure (QSG at ρ=.95: .696). The C family gives no replicated gain; C2's development gain did not replicate.
   112	* **Assessment: NOT SUPPORTED** as contributions. Kept as negative results within P2.
   113	
   114	## P6. LRMD (standalone restart monitor)
   115	
   116	* **Evidence.** Earlier registered null-safety failure. In the transfer run it is cheaper than gated+RMM on the family (+5.0 for gated+RMM vs LRMD), but its nulls were not re-tested here.
   117	* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE.** One fresh 1000-seed null run would decide it.
   118	
   119	---
   120	
   121	## Ranked recommendation for paper development
   122	
   123	1. **P2** as a benchmark/empirical paper: certified deployment under dependence. It has the broadest finding, every number is on fresh seeds, and its real-data relevance was measured.
   124	2. **P1** as a method paper on transferable restart monitoring for certified deployment. Run its two missing experiments first.
   125	3. Fold P3 to P5 into P2 as findings and negative results.

