docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:1:# Publication Opportunity Register (SOTA sprint, 2026-11-03)
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:3:This register follows the publishability addendum. Main-track contribution potential (A) and SOTA performance (B) are assessed separately.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:5:All numbers come from the sprint validation files in [validation/](validation/). Status uses three values: PROMISING, NEEDS EVIDENCE and NOT SUPPORTED. The result-to-claim review in [aris/](aris/) may revise these.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:9:## P1. A restart-mixture label-clock monitor that transfers across certified deployment methods (RMM)
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:21:* **Evidence.** All results are on fresh seeds, after the DISCERN `/4` fix ([transfer_results.md](validation/transfer_results.md), [rmm_certified_hosts.txt](validation/rmm_certified_hosts.txt)).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:26:  * Independent-null first-promotion safety holds on all three hosts (Wilson upper bound ≤ .0936 at 1000 seeds).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:27:  * Late improvement vs the best certified published method (Xu) is −15 to −27 per episode for every certified host, on V_late_600 and W_late_1000.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:31:  * Registered HR fails at 3/5 hosts. Model Picker and CAMS have no certificate for RMM to improve: their null promotion is .94 to .95 with or without RMM.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:32:  * Reversal and recurrence cost +20 to +37 vs the best certified method.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:35:  * On fresh real data RMM is much better than its host (gated: −48 to −156) but mixed vs the best certified method (2 wins, 3 losses). The fresh streams contain no late-improvement episode.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:36:* **Deviation to disclose.** Restricting the transfer claim to certificate hosts was decided after the registered 5-host HR failed. It is therefore a post-hoc scope, even though RMM's design rationale (adding monitoring to a certificate) predates it.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:38:  1. A registered fresh test restricted to certificate hosts, adding a fourth certificate family (e.g. choe_ramdas_eb or gated_adaptive_range).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:42:* **Positioning.** A transferable extension (addendum type 1) and failure-guided design (type 4), with a scoped SOTA in the late-improvement regime (type 3).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:44:* **(B) SOTA:** scoped, synthetic only. Late improvement beats the strongest certified published method by 15 to 27; nothing broader.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:47:## P2. Certified deployment under serial dependence: failure map and an impossibility-flavoured trade-off (Failure Atlas plus persistence study)
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:49:* **Research question.** Do anytime-valid deployment gates keep their false-promotion guarantee when label outcomes are serially dependent, as they are in real time-series streams? What does a dependence-robust certificate cost?
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:51:  * Every certified published port that has power exceeds the .0936 bound on a persistent marginal null. Run at 1000 seeds each:
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:58:  * The two that pass, original gated deployment and corrected DISCERN, almost never promote. A5b cost-dominates them on all 6 stationary alternatives.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:59:  * A gap-indexed sequential-universal-inference certificate (A1b/A5b) holds the bound for every ρ ≤ .95. It also holds on two out-of-class nulls (.048, .018). Its stationary cost is within +7.4 of the cheapest unsafe certified method.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:60:  * But it loses all power on real time-series streams. Fresh real data: +61 to +257 vs the best certified method. On metro it costs +99 with 0/16 episodes better, even though the challenger error is .047 vs .36.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:61:  * The mechanism is measured persistence: ρ̂ of .4 to .8 on the fresh streams, and up to .98 on exposed streams.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:68:  * The atlas measures marginal false promotion of deployment gates under controlled persistence, on one common budgeted, delayed simulator, against 13 published methods. It also shows the conditional-vs-marginal null distinction has decision consequences.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:74:* **Positioning.** A new evaluation methodology plus a substantive empirical finding (addendum types 5 and 6), possibly a datasets-and-benchmarks track.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:76:* **(B) SOTA:** A5b is the lowest-cost method among those that hold the bound at ρ ≤ .95 on synthetic data. It is not SOTA on real data.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:84:  * Native null promotion of 64 to 100% falls to .014 to .016 on V_null for all three hosts at 1000 seeds.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:85:  * Fresh real data, D2 (CAMS) vs the best certified method:
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:88:    * metro −2.7 (14/16)
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:92:  * Registered HD fails. Holm-significant wins vs the best certified method number 0, 2 and 2 of 5.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:93:  * D2's first-validation 4/5 did not replicate on new seeds.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:97:* **(A) Main-track potential:** low as a method. It is useful as the M2 benchmark finding inside P2.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:98:* **(B) SOTA:** not established.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:103:* **Evidence.** Reversal cost vs host: B2 −8.9 and −8.4; B5 −12.0 and −17.8 (fresh seeds).
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:105:  * One host only, in the RMM line, which fails ρ=.8.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:106:  * Reversal is still +20 or more vs the best certified method (discern_style), because that method never promotes.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:111:* **Evidence.** Earlier Atlas v2 work: stationary gains bought with dependence-safety failure (QSG at ρ=.95: .696). The C family gives no replicated gain; C2's development gain did not replicate.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:116:* **Evidence.** Earlier registered null-safety failure. In the transfer run it is cheaper than gated+RMM on the family (+5.0 for gated+RMM vs LRMD), but its nulls were not re-tested here.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:117:* **Assessment: NEEDS EVIDENCE.** One fresh 1000-seed null run would decide it.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:123:1. **P2** as a benchmark/empirical paper: certified deployment under dependence. It has the broadest finding, every number is on fresh seeds, and its real-data relevance was measured.
docs/sota_sprint/publication_register.md:124:2. **P1** as a method paper on transferable restart monitoring for certified deployment. Run its two missing experiments first.
